{
  "result": "The laundromat on Fifth Street had a shelf of books nobody seemed to take home. There was a guide to raising orchids, a mystery with its final page missing, and an atlas that still showed a country my mother used to live in. On Sundays, while my clothes turned behind the glass, I opened the atlas to the same page.\n\nOne afternoon a woman sorting socks beside me leaned over and tapped a blue bend in the river. \u201cThat should be farther east,\u201d she said. \u201cUnless the river moved.\u201d\n\n\u201cDo rivers do that?\u201d\n\n\u201cThis one did. My uncle went to bed with a vegetable garden and woke up with a boat landing.\u201d\n\nShe folded a sock into its partner. I asked her whether she knew the town where my mother was born. She said she knew the bakery there, or at least the bakery that had been there when bread cost less than the paper they wrapped it in. Then she asked if my mother still made the little plum dumplings everybody made in September.\n\nI had never heard of the dumplings. That evening I called my mother, who said of course she knew how to make them. She had stopped because my father hated cooked fruit. She had assumed I did too.\n\nThe next Sunday I brought six dumplings to the laundromat in a container with a cracked lid. The woman took one, bit into it, and asked if my mother was the one who always left the pits.",
